#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/id #uuid "2f27b473-e10a-5e77-8bb2-d8ae5d0ecf4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10037" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $50$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.5,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 3.355) = 0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=50, \\qquad\nn = 9, \\qquad\ns = 1.5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 3.355) = 0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-1,\\alpha/2} = t_{8,0.005} = 3.355.\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 50 & \\pm 1.678\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "c2e2bcbc-ab4b-5a0a-a803-cb98326017ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-10040" :question/problem "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $60 \\, \\textrm{mph}$ on a standard test car.   A sample of $81$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $132\\, \\textrm{ ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}.$ Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu$ of these tires.    \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2.$ However, the sample size $n = 81 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm  t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=132 \\, \\textrm{ft}, \\qquad\nn = 81, \\qquad\ns = 5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value $t_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T > t_{80, 0.025}) = 0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/d5/d513fbeee39a42a5e897c1b171d7e820196066517c5aa1c6c12549a98ce00ddd.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that $t_{80,0.025}=1.990{:}$ \n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}80}$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.990}$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}132 & \\pm 1.99\\cdot \\frac{5.4}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 132 & \\pm 1.194\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/id #uuid "7446f45b-6d41-5a11-8006-853b38fa5142" :question/math-academy-id "e-10047" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $30,$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.66) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2.$ However, the sample size $n = 100 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm  t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=30, \\qquad \nn = 100, \\qquad\ns = 8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.66) = 0.05.$ As a result,  \n\n$$\nt_{n-1,\\alpha/2} = t_{99,0.05} = 1.66.\n$$\n\n![](images/8d/8d4cd3a76fffee915059c849d3bac4ca395deb3e2cfe5ab1701e9ee4737b4401.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 30 & \\pm 1.328\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a22da69-4c5a-57b0-8f3c-1a6dbcece4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pm 3.925\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501540} {:answer/id #uuid "376a2dce-046e-5e42-a479-11f7c9e9d10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pm 1.962\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501566} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0430a5-38b2-5298-951f-2121518d9e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pm 3.925\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501576} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6e7dfb-0ac9-59a1-8797-a08ebedff424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501582} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a6c68-6420-5739-a652-82cc2f49f035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pm 7.152\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f6e7dfb-0ac9-59a1-8797-a08ebedff424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501582} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a298a57-d38a-51dd-8fbd-ddef7664c294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a4751d0-f8ad-5ca6-9ca9-6da91f8c5dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-142395" :question/problem "A farmer grows a particular variety of apples. He sampled $81$ apples from the harvest and found that the sample mean weight of apples is $120\\,\\text{g}$ and the sample standard deviation is $18\\,\\text{g}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire harvest. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=120\\,\\text{g},\\,n=81,\\,s=18\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{80,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/69/69f7cbb0e67e69c2ef95f9033f41ecd37a0ed21de94bcb903af17284aeb01155.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{80,0.005}=2.639:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $80$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & \\pm 2.639\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ 120 & \\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e9d1eb-a1d6-5f2e-9ffa-84f52aa385fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\pm 3.212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501499} {:answer/id #uuid "40f5bce4-7c32-53bf-862f-b2d2ea8f79b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\pm 6.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501585} {:answer/id #uuid "a34d9c75-e0b4-5580-94b7-f7a19bfeccb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\pm 7.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501779} {:answer/id #uuid "bf308865-2733-5a23-ba5e-bbeb4af2ef6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99\\pm 3.212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501851} {:answer/id #uuid "e28abb86-7056-5dd2-8dc2-888c83ced68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99\\pm 7.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40f5bce4-7c32-53bf-862f-b2d2ea8f79b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\pm 6.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501585} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8d330b-a65f-526a-88ee-75aa78b19a07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc8102d8-eaf2-5af9-98b6-20a38a45681f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142448" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $42$ and the sample standard deviation is $6,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=42,\\,n=9,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 42 & \\pm 6.71\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32de77ab-6c1d-5a97-8cb0-561563d33d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pm 2.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501560} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad098dc-7f2c-5437-8222-2899d151ab9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pm 3.506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501758} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd21b09-a056-56e6-ac86-61501d334558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pm 3.506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501759} {:answer/id #uuid "c538e266-2b11-5784-8d91-7aa3595545cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pm 2.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501859} {:answer/id #uuid "c5407ccc-e312-5feb-94a8-95e28811eacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pm 1.753" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad098dc-7f2c-5437-8222-2899d151ab9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pm 3.506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501758} :answer-field/id #uuid "213abdf6-e2d7-5205-ae3f-9d57185fc89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9109bebb-ab30-51e2-97b6-5e0e69a9d363" :question/math-academy-id "q-142449" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b5037831d90f93fb6b8b0603655162e4f362a638b35c569f7c5e07007974b3d.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $50,$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.753)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=50,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.753)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.05}=1.753.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 50\\pm 1.753\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 50\\pm 3.506\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0650c1b6-ebba-5d47-b139-03e824ee2178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\pm 0.448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501481} {:answer/id #uuid "312b5a90-f9ff-5cd3-a397-fb30d50599fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501554} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e89b00-8ba0-5ba2-8bc4-b3de1b014e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.5\\pm 2.617" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501787} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ce0fd-17c4-56cf-9b44-09ab31b94be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\pm 0.548" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501790} {:answer/id #uuid "b84f0546-d5eb-5ef1-8d85-f69877397c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\pm 0.393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b84f0546-d5eb-5ef1-8d85-f69877397c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\pm 0.393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501833} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a91344-29c8-5858-8b96-774cbbd1ece8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b03a1f2-3bac-59ff-a005-6c994dad1ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-142505" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=121$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.65,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.617)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $120$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=121\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12.5,\\,n=121,\\,s=1.65.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.617)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{120,0.005}=2.617.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18456a4a686aae65f078015f1669cad8e1316b66166567f22df35345ebd87fa4.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.5 & \\pm 2.617\\cdot \\frac{1.65}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\ 12.5 & \\pm 0.393\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c10d15-e976-5984-8aa6-6c11525b58bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225\\pm 1.319" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501479} {:answer/id #uuid "8a23c656-13bd-5339-8580-b3bf92d9bf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\pm 4.122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501730} {:answer/id #uuid "941b3c53-f51f-5225-8e09-99e78b4ed268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\pm 13.189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501751} {:answer/id #uuid "aec2e128-f4aa-5c9d-9489-2ac45fc400b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225\\pm 4.122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501811} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fd3412-40e3-568d-9e28-0ca3f5f3b050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\pm 5.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3fd3412-40e3-568d-9e28-0ca3f5f3b050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\pm 5.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501826} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe9a2f0e-9ecf-5c31-b505-d6c46aeae913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ae17796-447a-5d04-9973-717e1d847688" :question/math-academy-id "q-142506" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=225$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $98,$ and the sample standard deviation is $30,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.598)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $224$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=225\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=98,\\,n=225,\\,s=30.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.598)=0.005.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{224,0.005}=2.598.\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b711e6fe35f64d886472fa38c5a65afd7473b344949d7effd49405946f3cee5.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & \\pm 2.598\\cdot \\frac{30}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ 98 & \\pm 5.196\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-poi