t's $t$-distribution with $255$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=256\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=105,\\,n=256,\\,s=36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.969)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{255,0.025}=1.969.\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d108130264501813e8bfed332f345d8d005095127c5d9d8e169a1c2ae83b2ec7.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}105 & \\pm 1.969\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{256}} \\\\ 105 & \\pm 4.430\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a4386b-2b97-59a9-8364-19b069a3dffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\pm 2.144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501650} {:answer/id #uuid "7646f297-8c51-517d-bde6-5c799274e0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57\\pm 2.144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501685} {:answer/id #uuid "bb647bb7-56bb-5640-8415-c8b33e620036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\pm 1.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501837} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc4a441-63c0-551c-81a2-0c4aefefca13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57\\pm 0.536" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501941} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ccec8d-661c-5c0e-96c8-d2eb4c7a9ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\pm 3.722" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7646f297-8c51-517d-bde6-5c799274e0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57\\pm 2.144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501685} :answer-field/id #uuid "6957eb55-ecd2-59f6-bebd-02343910a899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0647362-6c93-5a80-87bb-0e4349d8ef9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142515" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=400$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $57,$ and the sample standard deviation is $26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.649)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $399$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=57,\\,n=400,\\,s=26.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.649)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{399,0.05}=1.649.\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d2cc71801ad3826dddfb670518ad038ccbf11f363569896fe3c3b673e5c58a6.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}57 & \\pm 1.649\\cdot \\frac{26}{\\sqrt{400}} \\\\ 57 & \\pm 2.144\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75fad598-5541-56fa-b53a-a91cb54eaa0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pm 0.462" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501683} {:answer/id #uuid "93b5491c-6aa1-5900-81e9-ad3ec4f7dae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.849" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501750} {:answer/id #uuid "ab01bf03-621b-5f1b-906d-1df991599f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 3.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501805} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f4c0b9-3e9c-50a4-845e-b51b352ed73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.462" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501834} {:answer/id #uuid "cfca92cd-2321-565c-b98f-3c04a14a8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.924" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfca92cd-2321-565c-b98f-3c04a14a8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.924" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501879} :answer-field/id #uuid "80164f95-be54-5d4a-8c56-3abf9a42e850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d606c2e-8088-532b-9f1f-331fecee3381" :question/math-academy-id "q-142518" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=81$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12,$ and the sample standard deviation is $5,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.664)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $80$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12,\\,n=81,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.664)=0.05.$ As a result,  \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{80,0.05}=1.664.\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bf3a9f49bb4b4fb24d4fb39bdf0b02743efd1c280875332e5f3bbc1539d953.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\pm 1.664\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ 12 & \\pm 0.924\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c062760-ad33-5060-805b-7922362c6616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.5\\pm 0.124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501572} {:answer/id #uuid "4de9d1fa-2114-5200-ac1f-74384ab0d808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35.5\\pm 1.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501607} {:answer/id #uuid "5af67d73-630d-5855-be78-1f49db4aac29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35.5\\pm 2.328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501635} {:answer/id #uuid "b29529d2-fe42-5676-82dd-5a515d0e03fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80.5\\pm 2.328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501824} {:answer/id #uuid "ca73b624-1cba-5a83-b12e-199698b3233b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pm 6.322" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4de9d1fa-2114-5200-ac1f-74384ab0d808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35.5\\pm 1.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501607} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3ddbbb-774a-5798-bdcc-84bb9aeba43b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aecd358b-06cc-5c20-9edb-c893e2b56662" :question/math-academy-id "q-142571" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c9b1e09734e74c098fd5e91eb3c72079157c3a69b1bb1d039bb46e823e5b7b6.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $35.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $3,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.064)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=35.5,\\,n=25,\\,s=3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.064)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.025}=2.064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35.5 & \\pm 2.064\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 35.5 & \\pm 1.238\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e1155e-4080-5d86-9fb4-472204d0fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4\\pm 5.894" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501517} {:answer/id #uuid "4789ab41-6901-54eb-aa3f-53c0093afcea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 5.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501598} {:answer/id #uuid "a903a349-4713-507e-9844-98a6ac0b3509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6\\pm 5.849" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501797} {:answer/id #uuid "b1050771-aa4e-50b6-be4b-e655bb7f5f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4\\pm 2.947" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501819} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ace7cb-9051-5506-b8cb-1f60b97ef81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pm 5.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19e1155e-4080-5d86-9fb4-472204d0fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4\\pm 5.894" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501517} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6d766a-ea73-5a42-be0b-a9ac219d158b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ec3d52-24b4-5797-862b-64e2b932e1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-142574" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $5.4$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\le \\mu \\le \\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=5.4,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5.4 & \\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 5.4 & \\pm 5.894\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19649959-4003-53db-91d0-5d410eaa2707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61\\pm 6.361" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501516} {:answer/id #uuid "5297df39-68a6-5686-88c6-517cb9a39e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pm 6.136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501617} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5b0270-2dff-535f-9067-e5382d8aa019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pm 6.631" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501670} {:answer/id #uuid "77ca80e5-2fd1-549f-9694-19cd2d235c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pm 3.315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501691} {:answer/id #uuid "8635bc80-2d87-5a03-8019-42b20d00f065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pm 6.136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d5b0270-2dff-535f-9067-e5382d8aa019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pm 6.631" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501670} :answer-field/id #uuid "035310c3-afea-5fec-b2c8-a377fd8df157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c5ba2c5-2aab-5530-84f9-62121bb3609f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142701" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $45$ and the sample standard deviation is $9,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=45,\\,n=16,\\,s=9.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 45\\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 45\\pm 6.631\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03970a14-aff7-5c52-af6e-86f7e484a35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.48\\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501472} {:answer/id #uuid "38911557-7e16-582d-8b7f-6cb45159b13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1\\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501569} {:answer/id #uuid "6b30daa1-2d5c-55b7-a422-1ffc2ef92800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.58\\pm 0.18\\text{ grade points}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501666} {:answer/id #uuid "86a3d3a6-4ce1-5abf-ac49-d7437dd30bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pm 0.04\\text{ grade points}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501725} {:answer/id #uuid "ce95bb58-ce07-523a-8996-cfcfab471ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1\\pm 0.48\\text{ grade points}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38911557-7e16-582d-8b7f-6cb45159b13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1\\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501569} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c864314-d95a-52b4-bfa6-7db070ad0c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a68a1f9-858f-5d69-bc49-809a211decce" :question/math-academy-id "q-142917" :question/problem "A random sample of $100$ students from a particular university yields a mean GPA of $3.1$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.48.$ Find a  $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean GPA $\\mu $ of all students.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=3.1,\\,n=100,\\,s=0.48.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{99,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=100-1=99$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/1b/1bb4e66b68bbc1fb55a36abafb4f184fb6b25b16314e989a47fe6b3a835cea65.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{99,0.05}=1.660:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $1.660$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.1 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{0.48}{\\sqrt{100}}\\,\\text{grade points} \\\\ 3.1 & \\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "286823f5-e1ca-5ff4-b00f-3035726d945d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.5\\pm 1.092\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501537} {:answer/id #uuid "324f3c3c-0d5a-54d1-9898-12a9ac39319f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.5\\pm 0.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501558} {:answer/id #uuid "420d3790-6d42-5aa4-a5f8-46e7f063546b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.5\\pm 1.012\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501589} {:answer/id #uuid "65847e64-c39f-539f-8e40-4781fe4afa82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501654} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4d75c3-5101-5fb0-a2ee-63489e79479d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.5\\pm 0.512\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65847e64-c39f-539f-8e40-4781fe4afa82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501654} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aff946d-6fe9-5a23-b7aa-c4e702a4b97f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3b129d2-0cd5-58e9-b5e8-9079b387fb53" :question/math-academy-id "q-142921" :question/problem "The body mass index (BMI) of Dr. Lee's patients is normally distributed. A random sample of $9$ patients yields a mean BMI of $25.5\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean BMI $\\mu $ among all of Dr. Lee's patients. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=25.5\\,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/ce/ce7d5fc9bbf2c9e28a0862247e6f762cad8a718401828070e96041e7f82b1891.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.05}=1.860:$   \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $1.860$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 25.5\\pm 1.860\\cdot \\frac{2.6}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & 25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c7b8c6a-761f-5a82-a1c2-b4c5a35747db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.076\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501518} {:answer/id #uuid "22d45bab-0972-53aa-8775-f34db848eb48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.08\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501526} {:answer/id #uuid "76d09209-de2a-584f-84af-58436e90ac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 0.076\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501687} {:answer/id #uuid "8d89c99c-293f-5b5b-82ff-6fdc2e3a206c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 0.08\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501739} {:answer/id #uuid "d700fb9d-ad2a-5ff1-97fd-6f0cc927fc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d700fb9d-ad2a-5ff1-97fd-6f0cc927fc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8\\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501893} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e42767-966c-51b4-b910-abefd154b0bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c887832-1efe-5629-bd9f-7e1600f20497" :question/math-academy-id "q-143026" :question/problem "A thread manufacturer tests the tensile strength of a sample containing $9$ pieces of a particular type of thread.\nThe mean tensile strength of the sample is $7.8\\,\\text{lb}$ with a standard deviation of $0.06\\,\\text{lb}.$\nAssuming that tensile strengths are normally distributed, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean tensile strength  $\\mu $ of this thread. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=7.8\\,\\text{lb},\\,n=9,\\,s=0.06\\,\\text{lb}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/11/1199336fffafda6d04b4e9562aec6b1bbc1726005a54180c8e8ed4935981ddd1.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.005}=3.355:$  \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $3.355$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{0.06}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{lb} \\\\ 7.8 & \\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cd20ee-aac1-5423-b371-9c47c16002c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501486} {:answer/id #uuid "7f58221a-5581-5cd8-b941-e7019e00303f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 0.209\\text{ dollars}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501708} {:answer/id #uuid "a23739fa-8c2c-585d-a041-01f577921abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501776} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c73524-2cab-5905-94b7-f488fb83a72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.834\\text{ dollars}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501916} {:answer/id #uuid "edbb0ce9-191b-52b5-9c96-2d993645ed7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.209\\text{ dollars}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07cd20ee-aac1-5423-b371-9c47c16002c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.15\\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501486} :answer-field/id #uuid "780401c4-b210-558d-b3c0-2045c484e1c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9cb4d01-534f-524c-85ee-ddefd795826f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143033" :question/problem "A sample of the different prices of a given product in $64$ randomly selected stores in a given state yields a mean price of $$37.15$ with a sample standard deviation of $$2.00.$  Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean price of the product  $\\mu $ across the state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=$37.15,\\,n=64,\\,s=$2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{63,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=64-1=63$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/43/43ddb73bfc30887b0de7b7debf98407dbcea330ebe55c485982d5c05c0681c9a.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{63,0.05}=1.669:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $1.669$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37.15 & \\pm 1.669\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{64}}\\text{ dollars} \\\\ 37.15 & \\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
